1
00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:04.070
For this lesson, you'll dive into
how you can improve your LLM

2
00:00:04.070 --> 00:00:07.173
evaluators and LLM as-a-Judge
evaluations within your agent.

3
00:00:07.874 --> 00:00:12.078
The idea here is that as you use
LLM as-a-Judge, you may want to improve

4
00:00:12.245 --> 00:00:16.182
the evaluators themselves, as
those are never going to be 100% accurate

5
00:00:16.349 --> 00:00:18.218
as you've learned in previous lessons.

6
00:00:18.218 --> 00:00:22.122
So this serves as a bonus lesson here
where you can learn how to improve those

7
00:00:22.122 --> 00:00:25.825
evaluators as you go through the process
of improving your agent itself.

8
00:00:26.559 --> 00:00:27.460
So if you think back

9
00:00:27.460 --> 00:00:31.297
to the previous slides lesson,
you might have noticed a case where

10
00:00:31.898 --> 00:00:36.898
you used both a code-based comparison
against ground truth and an LLM as-a-Judge

11
00:00:37.437 --> 00:00:40.707
function calling evaluation
to measure the performance of your router.

12
00:00:41.408 --> 00:00:42.709
And you might be thinking,

13
00:00:42.709 --> 00:00:46.146
why would you need to use two different
evaluators to evaluate the same thing?

14
00:00:46.713 --> 00:00:50.350
Especially because the code based
evaluator is going to be 100% accurate.

15
00:00:50.550 --> 00:00:52.185
So why would you add in the

16
00:00:52.185 --> 00:00:54.120
LLM as-a-Judge evaluator?

17
00:00:54.120 --> 00:00:56.823
Well, the answer there is
that you can use this technique

18
00:00:56.823 --> 00:01:01.461
to actually measure how effective
and how closely your LLM as-a-Judge

19
00:01:01.461 --> 00:01:06.461
evaluator aligns to your 100% accurate
code base comparison against ground truth.

20
00:01:07.734 --> 00:01:08.201
Because as you

21
00:01:08.201 --> 00:01:11.571
remember, LLM as-a-Judge is not 100%
accurate method.

22
00:01:12.138 --> 00:01:16.476
However, it can scale a lot further beyond
the comparison against ground truth.

23
00:01:16.676 --> 00:01:20.447
You could apply an LLM as-a-Judge eval
to all of the runs of your application

24
00:01:20.447 --> 00:01:24.584
if you want it to,
but it pays to know how accurate that LLM

25
00:01:24.584 --> 00:01:28.721
as-a-Judge is compared
to your 100% accurate evaluation method.

26
00:01:29.856 --> 00:01:34.294
So you can use experiments to actually
improve your LLM as-a-Judge itself.

27
00:01:35.095 --> 00:01:35.562
The experiments

28
00:01:35.562 --> 00:01:39.099
that you just learned about in a previous
lesson, can still be applied

29
00:01:39.332 --> 00:01:43.169
this time to your LLM as-a-Judge,
as opposed to to your agent itself.

30
00:01:43.770 --> 00:01:44.571
It's a little bit meta,

31
00:01:44.571 --> 00:01:48.174
but you can use the same techniques
to judge your judge in this case.

32
00:01:49.142 --> 00:01:49.776
So you could set up

33
00:01:49.776 --> 00:01:52.779
an experiment on the function
calling LLM judge.

34
00:01:53.546 --> 00:01:56.549
And you might have an example test case
that looks something like we see here

35
00:01:56.716 --> 00:01:57.684
where you have an input.

36
00:01:57.684 --> 00:02:00.920
That's which stores
have the best sales performance in 2021

37
00:02:01.421 --> 00:02:04.424
and then an output in this case
the database lookup.

38
00:02:04.657 --> 00:02:05.325
And the important thing

39
00:02:05.325 --> 00:02:09.562
is that everything in red
there is the input to your LLM judge,

40
00:02:09.896 --> 00:02:14.467
because you're having your LLM judge
judge the performance of that agent.

41
00:02:14.968 --> 00:02:17.403
So everything in red there is the input.

42
00:02:17.403 --> 00:02:20.807
And then you'd have your expected output
in this case is correct.

43
00:02:21.007 --> 00:02:24.010
So that expected output
is your ground truth data.

44
00:02:24.144 --> 00:02:27.247
And then you could set up the experiment
to test different versions of your

45
00:02:27.514 --> 00:02:28.915
LLM as-a-Judge prompt.

46
00:02:28.915 --> 00:02:30.483
You might change
some of the wording there.

47
00:02:30.483 --> 00:02:33.620
Or you could add things
like few shot examples.

48
00:02:33.620 --> 00:02:37.390
So you could add in some examples
of previous judgments made

49
00:02:37.390 --> 00:02:40.927
that were correct to help align
the judge into that prompt as well.

50
00:02:41.828 --> 00:02:44.464
And then you can evaluate
the performance of your LLM as-a-Judge

51
00:02:44.464 --> 00:02:47.534
using the code-based comparison
against ground truth.

52
00:02:47.734 --> 00:02:51.638
In this case, comparing to that correct
or incorrect expected output label.

53
00:02:52.405 --> 00:02:54.774
And you can use the same approach
for another LLM as-a-Judge

54
00:02:54.774 --> 00:02:56.576
that you're using, in this case
the one you're using

55
00:02:56.576 --> 00:02:59.279
to evaluate analysis clarity.

56
00:02:59.279 --> 00:03:00.580
And so in that example,

57
00:03:00.580 --> 00:03:03.550
you might have an input
that looks something like you see here:

58
00:03:03.550 --> 00:03:06.553
in 2021 the stores that perform the best,
yada yada.

59
00:03:07.187 --> 00:03:11.324
And then the expected output of the
analysis is clearer because of x, y and z.

60
00:03:12.358 --> 00:03:15.528
And this time
you might judge different types of models

61
00:03:15.528 --> 00:03:18.531
that are being used to create that
judge label.

62
00:03:18.698 --> 00:03:20.266
You could also test different LLM

63
00:03:20.266 --> 00:03:23.269
as a judge prompts similar
to what you did in the previous example.

64
00:03:23.503 --> 00:03:26.506
Now the question here though becomes
how do you evaluate that output?

65
00:03:26.539 --> 00:03:30.376
Because while you have expected output,
it's no longer a clean

66
00:03:30.376 --> 00:03:32.445
just correct or incorrect label.

67
00:03:32.445 --> 00:03:36.282
It's this expected output of the analysis
is clear because of something.

68
00:03:37.116 --> 00:03:39.552
And so what if your LLM as-a-Judge
comes back with

69
00:03:39.552 --> 00:03:42.789
the analysis is easy to understand
because of x, y, and z.

70
00:03:43.189 --> 00:03:44.090
That should be correct.

71
00:03:44.090 --> 00:03:46.593
But you can't just compare
those strings exactly.

72
00:03:46.593 --> 00:03:49.128
That's where you can use something
like semantic similarity

73
00:03:49.128 --> 00:03:52.332
to compare the meaning
of each of those strings in a numeric way,

74
00:03:52.632 --> 00:03:55.969
as opposed to doing a direct comparison
between output, unexpected output.

75
00:03:56.603 --> 00:04:00.106
So in this lesson, you've learned
how you can measure and improve your LLM

76
00:04:00.106 --> 00:04:00.607
as-a-Judge

77
00:04:00.607 --> 00:04:03.343
evals using structured experiments.

78
00:04:03.343 --> 00:04:06.646
And your next and final lesson,
you'll learn about moving your agents

79
00:04:06.646 --> 00:04:09.449
into production
and monitoring those agents in production,

80
00:04:09.449 --> 00:04:12.518
as well as wrap up all the lessons
that you've learned here in the course.

